{
	title: 'Multiline prompt: rows, goal column and a scrolling box',
	spec: `Up and Down move between the visual rows of the wrapped prompt and keep a goal column across moves. Columns are display columns: wide characters count two, a literal tab reaches the next 4-column stop, and a column that falls inside a wide glyph or tab snaps to its nearer edge. step() stays pure: the terminal passes the prompt's content width, and the layout used for moving is the one it draws (the wrap helper in common/strings.ts). On the top row Up goes to the start of the text, on the bottom row Down to its end (input history, task 91, takes these edges over). Alt-Up/Down go to the start/end of the whole text. Home/End and Ctrl-A/E still act on the logical line.
The terminal prompt box shows at most 10 rows (a setting in src/common/settings.ts) and scrolls inside itself: its viewport moves only when the cursor leaves it, keeping one row of context when it must; the rules above and below the box show how many rows are hidden (up N, down N). Ctrl-= and Ctrl-Up grow the box by a row, Ctrl-- and Ctrl-Down shrink it (never below what auto height would give); sending resets it. When text exactly fills a row the cursor stays in the right padding rather than wrapping early. Literal tabs are drawn as spaces to the next 4-column stop, never as raw tab bytes (tasks/cc/terminal.md, Text tab characters).
An empty prompt shows a quieter example request on the cursor row, rotating per turn. Its hue follows the prompt text; its lightness is the box lightness + 0.3 rounded to tenths, and its chroma is capped in steps by the prompt's chroma (at most 0.08 normally, 0.09 at prompt chroma 0.16–0.19, 0.12 at 0.2 or higher). With the preview palettes the resulting colours are HAL oklch(0.5 0.08 225), phosphor oklch(0.5 0.09 215), nostromo oklch(0.5 0.12 145); each has about 3:1 contrast against its box, legible but distinct from typed text. The web uses the same derived colour (--faint of .input), and the terminal fits it to sRGB. This is the one exception to the readable-text rule (tasks/README.md). On the web it is Hal's own overlay, not the textarea's placeholder, so it fades: out in 80 ms as typing starts, back in 150 ms when the box empties (ease-out, none under reduced motion); the Send button likewise eases its colour (150 ms) as it turns on and off (artifact old-placeholders.ts; one list for any project, another when cwd is the Hal repo).
Web: the textarea wraps and moves natively. The intro tab (hal/intro) shows no example: it cannot take requests.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"old-prompt.test.ts in task 1x has the cases this task must keep: 'up preserves visual column across tab-indented lines', 'vertical movement chooses the nearest edge of a tab', 'tabs participate in prompt wrapping at their displayed width', 'cursor stays in right padding when text exactly fills prompt row', 'down keeps existing prompt viewport when cursor remains visible', 'ctrl-= and ctrl-- resize the prompt editor height'.",
		"Mind the cc notes on wrapping: frame.clean() keeps UTF-16 offsets unchanged because the prompt cursor is an offset into the raw text, and wordWrap measures columns per row. The editor's row starts must match what the frame draws, or Up lands on the wrong character; test both from one layout.",
		'Ctrl-Up/Down may be taken by macOS Mission Control; Ctrl-= / Ctrl-- are the reliable pair.',
		'The layout is src/common/prompt-layout.ts: wordWrap rows kept as source offset ranges (a dropped break space is skipped; a dropped space at a line end gets its own empty row so the cursor after it sits on a new row). promptView.layoutPrompt (frame and form fields) draws from those ranges, so drawing and Up/Down share one layout.',
		'A row broken mid-word ends at the offset the next row starts at, which shows on the next row; offsetAt stops before its last glyph so Up from a row end stays on the row above.',
